  "slug": "spatial-hi-18ec",
  "summary": "Reference-free and simulation-based inference methods now extract tumor and cell-type properties from sparse single-cell, proteomic, and bulk datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational recovery of tumor and cell-type information where conventional references or complete measurements are absent or sparse. REFCON infers copy number from single-cell tumor transcriptomes without a normal-cell reference. ssSTICI applies sample-specific transformer imputation to sparse scRNA-seq cell-variant alignments to improve genetic cell type recovery. SoftHybrid addresses missingness in single-cell proteomics by weighting MAR and MNAR strategies according to an estimated MNAR probability. DeconX extends this logic to bulk RNA-seq, generating pseudo-cells from deconvolution residuals to estimate reference-missing cell types. The cfDNA simulation study, spanning 480 million in silico samples across 10 cancers, provides the population-scale counterpart by quantifying how coverage and base quality set the lowest detectable tumor fraction. What is new is the reference-free, missingness-aware framing applied across modalities, rather than any single assay.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-07"
}
